128	        "mask": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 0, "description": "The bits the claim is about: the step compares `value & mask` against `equals`, which is how a plan asserts one flag of a status word without stating every other bit of it. Applied to the unsigned reading, because a mask selects bits while a sign is a reading of the whole width; that is why `signed` is refused beside it rather than quietly ignored. The report carries both the value read and the masked value it was judged on." },
129	        "signed": { "type": "boolean", "default": false, "description": "Which of the two integer readings this claim is about. `debug_symbol_value` returns `value_unsigned` and `value_signed` because the same bytes are honestly both, and above the top bit the two disagree, so a plan asserting a counter that can go negative says so here instead of writing the two's complement of the number it means. Defaults to false, which is the reading `hex` shows in memory order." }
